      "To see how we get hooked you have to understand the 'sleeping partner', the unconscious mind, which absorbs what society tells us; ninety-nine percent of what we are is moulded. From our earliest years it is fed the message that cigarettes relax us and give courage: the condemned man's last cigarette, the wounded soldier handed one, and films, sport and advertising that find ways around the bans. Health scares do not undo this. The strongest force is the smoker himself, who has to believe the habit is worth it, which is why everyone cites an Uncle Fred who smoked forty a day and lived to eighty. Smokers are not weak; most are strong people under stress or in monotonous jobs, and governments gain billions from them.\n\nStart building resistance, as you would with a second-hand car dealer, and look behind the glossy packet at what is inside. The author calls himself the biggest idiot of all: he watched his father destroyed and begged never to become a smoker, then reached a hundred a day and could not change a light bulb without lighting up. Addiction makes the brainwashing worse; the worst harm is not to health or pocket but the warping of a mind that searches for any excuse. Stop saying 'give up', which implies a sacrifice, and say stop, quit or escape. It is the smoker, not the non-smoker, who is deprived, of health, energy, wealth, peace of mind, confidence, courage, self-respect, happiness and freedom, and all he gets in return is the illusion of regaining the calm non-smokers have all the time.",
