      'Self-imposed slavery',
      "People usually try to stop for health, money and social stigma, but part of the brainwashing is plain slavery. We fought to abolish slavery, yet smokers choose it: when allowed to smoke they wish they were not smokers, and when they are not allowed they are miserable. Most cigarettes are neither enjoyed nor even noticed; one only becomes precious after a gap, such as the first of the morning, or when cutting down or a ban forces a pause. The trend will only tighten, from public places to your car, home and garden. The author recalls searching strangers' houses for an ashtray, being told 'we would rather you didn't', and thinking through his own daughter's wedding only about getting outside for a drag. Smokers huddle together offering each other cigarettes as a little reward, but the non-smoker needs no reward and already has the calm the smoker is chasing. One of the great joys of being free is not spending half your life craving a cigarette and the other half wishing you did not. In a non-smoker's house it is not the self-righteous non-smoker who deprives you but the little monster.",
