      'The willpower method',
      "The author says stopping is ridiculously easy; it is the willpower method that makes it hard, because it makes you feel you are sacrificing something. We experiment with the first cigarettes, sure we can stop whenever we like, and are hooked before we notice, believing meals, company and stress relief depend on them, though the same cigarette tastes the same after a meal as in the morning. The first attempts come in a stressful moment over money or health; the pangs then add stress and the usual prop is gone, a triple blow, and the usual result is a compromise: cut down, or wait for a better time. Hoping to wake up one day not wanting to smoke does not work either; people who quit suddenly have usually had a shock or were already preparing.\n\nBefore he even starts, the smoker is steeped in the brainwashing of society, of his own addiction and of stories about how hard it is, so he begins with doom and gloom as if climbing Everest. After a few days his lungs clear and the money stays in his pocket, so his reasons fade, like the memory of a road accident that slows you only for a moment, while the unfed little monster sets off the big one and he starts hunting for excuses: life is too short, wrong time, I'm irritable, I am a confirmed smoker. He lights up and decides he lacks willpower, but he has only changed his mind. The misery is not withdrawal but doubt, and waiting for something to happen that never does. After about three weeks the physical craving is gone, but many test it with a cigarette and the nicotine restarts the voice; the occasional cigar does the same. Those who succeed this way stay vulnerable. Enjoyment never came into it: if we smoked for enjoyment, nobody would smoke more than one.",
