      "Substitutes such as chewing gum, sweets, peppermints, herbal cigarettes, potions and pills are all out: they make it harder, because using one says 'I need to smoke or fill the void', like giving in to a hijacker or a child's tantrum. The craving is for nicotine, not food, so a substitute only keeps you thinking about smoking. There is no substitute for nicotine, you do not need it, and cigarettes create the void rather than fill it; the quicker you teach your brain that you need neither smoking nor anything else in its place, the sooner you are free. Avoid every nicotine product too. The logic of nicotine replacement therapy is to break the habit first and cut the drug later, but smoking is addiction rather than habit and the physical pain is almost imperceptible, so it only prolongs the little monster and with it the big one; those who succeed with it do so despite it. Many people arrive at his clinics hooked on the gum.\n\nThe chief evil of substitutes is that they prolong the real problem, the brainwashing: needing one means 'I am making a sacrifice'. You will only swap one problem for another and end up fat and miserable. Casual smokers cling to the cigarette as a little reward at a break, but those were never rewards; they were like wearing tight shoes for the pleasure of taking them off. If you feel you must have a reward, the author jokes, wear shoes a size too small until your break. Real breaks will be even better without choking yourself. If your appetite improves and you put on a couple of pounds in the first days, do not worry; confidence will sort it out later. What you must not do is pick between meals, or you will get fat and miserable and never know when you have kicked it.",
