      "Before the final cigarette, check the two essentials: do you feel certain of success, and do you feel excitement rather than doom and gloom? If you have doubts, re-read the book. You never decided to fall into the trap, and to escape you must make a positive decision: make a solemn vow that, easy or difficult, you will never smoke another. The worst that can happen is that you fail, and then you are no worse off. Then follow the instructions: make the vow and mean it; smoke the last cigarette consciously, inhale deeply and ask where the pleasure is; put it out feeling 'Isn't it great! I'm free!', not 'I'm not allowed another'. For a few days a little saboteur will say 'I want a cigarette'. The body does not crave nicotine; only the brain does, and the feeling is an empty, insecure one that you can meet with 'Yippee, I'm a non-smoker' instead of craving for life.\n\nIt is only doubting and waiting that make it hard, and doubt puts you in a no-win position: miserable without a cigarette, even more miserable with one. What non-smokers have is no need or desire to smoke, and you get it the moment you put out the last one, so you are already a happy non-smoker. You will stay one if you never doubt your decision, do not wait to become one, do not try not to think about smoking or wait for the moment of revelation, use no substitutes, pity rather than envy smokers, and do not change your life just because you quit, since you have given up nothing. Whenever you think about smoking, in the next few days or for the rest of your life, think: Yippee, I'm a non-smoker!",
